/T (Q: What challenges have you faced as an international candidate, particularly regarding visa and language barriers? A: As an international candidate, both visa and language barriers are significant challenges. I was fortunate that I secured my first full-time position in Korea relatively quickly after graduation, since it was actually the first interview I ever had. However, after that, I experienced a much more difficult job search, especially when the market conditions worsened. I applied to many roles and tailored my resume for each position, but rejections were common. It can be easy to feel discouraged or desperate in that situation, but I chose to shift my approach toward networking instead.)
/E (Q: What challenges have you faced as an international candidate, particularly regarding visa and language barriers? A: As an international candidate, both visa and language barriers are significant challenges. I was fortunate that I secured my first full-time position in Korea relatively quickly after graduation, since it was actually the first interview I ever had. However, after that, I experienced a much more difficult job search, especially when the market conditions worsened. I applied to many roles and tailored my resume for each position, but rejections were common. It can be easy to feel discouraged or desperate in that situation, but I chose to shift my approach toward networking instead.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
1000 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H1
/P 305 0 R
/T (One key difference I noticed is that in Korea, it is generally more straightforward to apply directly to jobs, whereas in France, the process is much more dependent on networking. I would say it is about 50/50 between applications and referrals, and many of my classmates secured their jobs through connections rather than formal applications. Building genuine relationships became a crucial strategy for me.)
/E (One key difference I noticed is that in Korea, it is generally more straightforward to apply directly to jobs, whereas in France, the process is much more dependent on networking. I would say it is about 50/50 between applications and referrals, and many of my classmates secured their jobs through connections rather than formal applications. Building genuine relationships became a crucial strategy for me.)
/Pg 57 0 R
/K [4]
>>
